{"name":"Sheet Metal","hex":"#5e6063","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sheet metal","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Metal worked into a thin, flat sheet, used widely as construction material and raw material for a multitude of industrial products. It is thicker than foil and thinner than plate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sheet_metal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sheet metal","blocks":[{"text":"Sheet metal is metal formed into thin, flat pieces, usually by an industrial process."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Chrysler_Building_detail.jpg/330px-Chrysler_Building_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheet_metal"},{"title":"Metal","blocks":[{"text":"A metal is a material that, when polished or fractured, shows a lustrous appearance, and conducts electricity and heat relatively well. These properties are all associated with having electrons available at the Fermi level, as opposed to nonmetallic materials which do not. Metals are typically ductile and malleable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mild_steel_sheet_metal_close_up.jpg/500px-Mild_steel_sheet_metal_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mild_steel_sheet_metal_close_up.jpg/1280px-Mild_steel_sheet_metal_close_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mild_steel_sheet_metal_close_up.jpg/500px-Mild_steel_sheet_metal_close_up.jpg?utm_source=